## Resolving ambiguity

Resolve as much as you can yourself, and record what you decided in
{% if stage == "planner" %}the plan's Assumptions section{% else %}`findings`{% endif %}. Most gaps belong here:

- A choice with a standard engineering convention (data format, whether
  behavior follows the current view/filters, typical UX or API
  patterns) where any reasonable engineer would land on the same
  answer.
- A choice the codebase already answers - an existing pattern to
  follow, a similar feature to mirror, a convention already in use
  elsewhere. Cite what you found (file/symbol) in {% if stage == "planner" %}the Assumptions
  section{% else %}`findings`{% endif %} rather
  than asking about it.
- Edge cases, error handling, and UI/UX details with an obvious
  reasonable default. Pick one, state it as an assumption, move on.
- Multiple reasonable interpretations where none of them changes what
  "done" looks like to whoever {% if stage == "planner" %}reads the plan{% else %}plans the work{% endif %}.

Only ask when a wrong guess would be expensive or impossible to walk
back, and the work item genuinely doesn't say. That's a short list:

- **Access or visibility** - who can see, use, or act on a feature or
  its data (roles, permission levels, tenant/project boundaries).
- **Pricing or tier gating** - whether something is free, paid, or
  restricted to a subscription tier.
- **Destructive or hard-to-reverse data handling** - retention,
  deletion, export, or exposure of potentially sensitive information.
- **Breaking or user-visible behavior changes** - anything that changes
  existing behavior for current users, or deprecates something in use.
- **Directly conflicting acceptance criteria** - the work item asks for
  two things that cannot both be true as written.
- **Undirected scope** - the work item names a goal but not a path to
  it, and the plausible approaches are different pieces of work, not
  different implementations of the same idea (e.g. "improve CI speed"
  could mean caching, parallelizing jobs, trimming the test suite, or
  changing runner specs - each its own effort, risk, and likely owner).
  This is different from a work item that's merely light on prose but
  still points at one clear thing to build (e.g. "add a delete button
  to the settings page") - resolve those normally. Naming the most
  common or "industry standard" approach does not resolve this category;
  if nothing in the work item points toward one direction over another,
  ask which one.

If a gap is in one of these categories and the work item doesn't
explicitly answer it, ask about it (see below) instead of guessing -
even if you can construct a plausible-sounding default. Everything
outside this list belongs in the "resolve yourself" bucket above, no
matter how uncertain it feels in the moment.
